; GameYear@ToString, the one place the game builds the in-game date, re-emitted
; so that the month arrives as a name: "LP 7 Dec, early" for ＬＰ７年１２月前半.
;
; It resolves GameYearEraName, GameYearMonthName and GameYearHalfName by name,
; from patches/lp_date.jaf, so alice-tools has to be handed that file before
; this one or it stops with "Unable to resolve function". That .jaf carries all
; the English and the reasoning; this file is the wiring.
;
; A .jam rather than an override for one reason: GameYear keeps its four fields
; as properties -- <YearTitle>, <Year>, <Month>, <Half> -- and the .jaf compiler
; reaches none of them, so a `override string GameYear@ToString(void)` cannot
; read the month it has to name. Overriding the getters instead does not help
; either: Month::get returns an int, and the format string is what turns it into
; text.
;
; Save this file as UTF-8 with no BOM. The assembler reads a byte order mark as
; an instruction and stops on line 1 -- "Invalid instruction: ϻ" -- and an
; editor that defaults .jam to UTF-8-with-BOM will quietly put one back.
;
; The body is that function verbatim from
;
;   alice ain dump --function GameYear@ToString game/ain/Rance10.v1.04.ain
;
; with four things changed and nothing else touched; regenerate it the same way
; if the game version changes, since every number in it indexes one build of one
; .ain. It adds no local, which is what makes it possible at all -- --jam
; rewrites code and leaves the FUNC section alone, so a function keeps the
; locals it was compiled with.
;
;   the format      "%s%D年%D月%s" becomes "%s %d %s, %s": half-width digits,
;                   and the third field is a string now rather than a number
;   26185           YearTitle::get, its result passed through GameYearEraName
;   26191           Month::get, through GameYearMonthName, and S_MOD 2 -> S_MOD 4
;   26194           Half::get, through GameYearHalfName, where the original
;                   called 26203 HalfText::get and formatted the 前半 that
;                   returns. Handing the int over instead leaves s[3111] and
;                   s[3112] out of the chain entirely, which is the point: those
;                   two are also the name of a CG the war-result screen asks for.
;                   It leaves both HalfText getters with no callers, the way
;                   表示規模 has none.
;
; The literals are written here rather than in
; patches/system_cherry_picks.v1.04.ain.txt because what this function pushes is
; what the four views show. s[3678] and s[3679] keep their Japanese and are read
; by nothing else.
;
; Nothing needs clipping. Measured with gameTextWidth over every turn in
; archives/Rance10EX_v1_04/36_ＬＰ情報.x, the widest English is "RA 15 May, late"
; and it is narrower than the widest Japanese, ＬＰ７年１０月前半, at all four
; sites: 419 against 441 on the turn-start banner, 193 against 207 in the corner
; frame, 129 against 153 in the ADV box.
FUNC 26200
	PUSHSTRUCTPAGE
	PUSH 26201
	.STRUCTREF GameYear m_chapter
	CALLMETHOD 1
	IFNZ 0x404358
	JUMP 0x4043b0
0x404358:
	S_PUSH "%s %d %s, %s"
	PUSHSTRUCTPAGE
	PUSH 26185
	CALLMETHOD 0
	CALLFUNC GameYearEraName
	S_MOD 4
	PUSHSTRUCTPAGE
	PUSH 26188
	CALLMETHOD 0
	S_MOD 2
	PUSHSTRUCTPAGE
	PUSH 26191
	CALLMETHOD 0
	CALLFUNC GameYearMonthName
	S_MOD 4
	PUSHSTRUCTPAGE
	PUSH 26194
	CALLMETHOD 0
	CALLFUNC GameYearHalfName
	S_MOD 4
	RETURN
0x4043b0:
	S_PUSH "XX ??/??"
	RETURN
	S_PUSH ""
	RETURN
ENDFUNC GameYear@ToString
